      <tr><td><b>Mid-Size Bi-Color LED Panel + Softbox</b></td><td>₹7,000 – ₹12,000</td><td>Panel with softbox diffusion</td><td>90–95 CRI</td><td>DC adapter, some battery</td><td>Interviews, YouTube setups, small product shoots</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Entry COB Light + Reflector Dish</b></td><td>₹8,000 – ₹14,000</td><td>Single COB source, Bowens-mount accessories</td><td>95+ CRI (higher-end COBs)</td><td>Mains-powered (AC)</td><td>Single hard-controlled key, modifier flexibility</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>5-in-1 Reflector (fill only)</b></td><td>₹300 – ₹700</td><td>Passive bounce/diffusion</td><td>N/A (bounces existing light)</td><td>None</td><td>Fill light, softening shadows, budget bounce</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>1. Compact Bi-Color LED Panel — The Default First Light</h2>
  <div class="cam-card">
    <div class="cam-head">
      <div>
        <span class="rank-pill">#1 · BEST ALL-ROUNDER</span>
        <div class="cam-title">Compact Bi-Color LED Panel (3200K–5600K)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cam-price">₹4,500 – ₹8,000</div>
    </div>
    <div class="spec-strip">
      <span class="spec-chip">Bi-color 3200K-5600K</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">CRI 90+</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Battery + adapter power</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Barn doors / diffuser included</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Lightweight stand-mount</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Dimmable</span>
    </div>
    <p>This is the category most beginners should start in. These panels are portable, run off both batteries and a DC adapter (useful when you're shooting somewhere without easy mains access), and are simple enough that you're not fighting the gear while you're learning to see light. Look specifically for a listing that states a CRI or TLCI number — if a product page doesn't mention CRI at all, that's usually a sign it's a budget diode array not worth trusting for skin tones.</p>
    <p><strong>Colorist's take:</strong> This is the light I'd hand a beginner on day one. It's forgiving, portable, and if you buy one with genuinely rated CRI 90+, it'll get you 80% of the way to a clean, gradeable image before you've spent a rupee on modifiers. Don't chase the highest lux number in the listing — a panel claiming huge brightness at this price often does it by sacrificing color accuracy.</p>
    <div class="pc-grid">
      <div class="pc-box pro"><h4>Pros</h4><ul>
        <li>Portable, battery-friendly, easy to learn on</li>
        <li>Bi-color removes the need for gels in most situations</li>
        <li>Usually comes with a basic diffuser and barn doors</li>
        <li>Cheapest entry into genuinely controllable lighting</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="pc-box con"><h4>Cons</h4><ul>
        <li>Flat, less directional light than a COB + modifier setup</li>
        <li>Cheaper units may overstate CRI — verify before buying</li>
        <li>Bundled stands are often the weakest part of the kit</li>
        <li>Limited output for larger rooms or overpowering daylight</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
    <div class="buy-row">
      <a class="buy-btn" href="https://www.amazon.in/s?k=bi-color+LED+video+light+panel+CRI+95" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check on Amazon</a>
      <a class="buy-btn alt" href="https://www.flipkart.com/search?q=bi-color%20LED%20video%20light%20panel%20CRI%2095" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check on Flipkart</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>2. Mid-Size LED Panel with Softbox — The Interview Setup Pick</h2>
  <div class="cam-card">
    <div class="cam-head">
      <div>
        <span class="rank-pill">#2 · BEST FOR INTERVIEWS</span>
        <div class="cam-title">Mid-Size Bi-Color LED Panel + Softbox Kit</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cam-price">₹7,000 – ₹12,000</div>
    </div>
    <div class="spec-strip">
      <span class="spec-chip">Bi-color 3200K-5600K</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">CRI 90-95</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Included softbox diffusion</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Stronger output than compact panels</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Stand included</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Remote/app dimming on some models</span>
    </div>
    <p>If your primary use case is a fixed setup — a talking-head channel, podcast-style interviews, or a home studio corner — this is the step up worth taking. The larger panel size combined with a softbox gives you noticeably softer, more wrapping light on the face than a small bare panel, which matters more for close-up shots than raw brightness does.</p>
    <p><strong>Colorist's take:</strong> Softness of the light source relative to the subject's face size is what separates "lit" from "well lit." A bigger panel with proper diffusion at a reasonable distance beats a small bright panel every time for close-up talking-head work — the shadows wrap instead of cutting hard across the face.</p>
    <div class="pc-grid">
      <div class="pc-box pro"><h4>Pros</h4><ul>
        <li>Softer, more flattering light for close-ups</li>
        <li>Usually a proper stand included in the bundle</li>
        <li>Good balance of price and light quality</li>
        <li>Some models offer app-based or remote dimming</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="pc-box con"><h4>Cons</h4><ul>
        <li>Bulkier — less ideal for travel or run-and-gun shoots</li>
        <li>Usually mains-powered only, limiting location flexibility</li>
        <li>Takes up more space to set up and store</li>
        <li>Uses most of the ₹15,000 budget on one item</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
    <div class="buy-row">
      <a class="buy-btn" href="https://www.amazon.in/s?k=LED+softbox+video+light+kit+bi-color" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check on Amazon</a>
      <a class="buy-btn alt" href="https://www.flipkart.com/search?q=LED%20softbox%20video%20light%20kit%20bi-color" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check on Flipkart</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Entry-Level COB Light — The Directional Control Pick</h2>
  <div class="cam-card">
    <div class="cam-head">
      <div>
        <span class="rank-pill">#3 · MOST VERSATILE LONG-TERM</span>
        <div class="cam-title">Entry-Level COB Light with Reflector Dish</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cam-price">₹8,000 – ₹14,000</div>
    </div>
    <div class="spec-strip">
      <span class="spec-chip">Single COB source</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Bowens-mount accessory system</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">CRI 95+ on better units</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Mains-powered</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Focusable/dish-controlled beam</span>
      <span class="spec-chip">Fixed or bi-color depending on model</span>
    </div>
    <p>COB lights sit slightly outside the typical "beginner kit" bundle but deserve a mention because they're the better long-term investment if you plan to keep upgrading your lighting setup. A single COB with a Bowens-mount reflector dish gives you a much more directional, shapeable beam, and you can later add a softbox, a grid, or barn doors onto the same mount without replacing the light itself.</p>
    <p><strong>Colorist's take:</strong> If you already know you're going to be lighting seriously in six months, buy the COB now instead of buying a cheap panel and rebuying later. The Bowens mount ecosystem means your one light grows with you — softbox for interviews, grid for dramatic falloff, dish alone for hard punchy light.</p>
    <div class="pc-grid">
      <div class="pc-box pro"><h4>Pros</h4><ul>
        <li>Directional, controllable beam out of the box</li>
        <li>Bowens mount lets you add modifiers over time</li>
        <li>Often better build quality and heat management</li>
        <li>Scales into a "real" lighting kit as you grow</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="pc-box con"><h4>Cons</h4><ul>
        <li>Mains-powered only — no battery operation on most models</li>
        <li>Bare bulb needs a modifier to be usable for faces</li>
        <li>Some budget COBs are fixed daylight only, not bi-color</li>
        <li>Uses your entire budget on one fixture, no fill left over</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
    <div class="buy-row">
      <a class="buy-btn" href="https://www.amazon.in/s?k=COB+LED+video+light+with+reflector+bowens+mount" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check on Amazon</a>
      <a class="buy-btn alt" href="https://www.flipkart.com/search?q=COB%20LED%20video%20light%20bowens%20mount" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check on Flipkart</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>How to Build Your ₹15,000 Setup</h2>
  <p>Here's how I'd actually split the budget rather than buying a bundled "3-light kit":</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>₹8,000–₹10,000</strong> — One bi-color LED panel or entry COB with CRI 90+, this is your key light.</li>
    <li><strong>₹1,500–₹2,500</strong> — A sturdy light stand if one isn't already included with the light.</li>
    <li><strong>₹300–₹700</strong> — A 5-in-1 reflector for fill and bounce — this replaces your second light for now.</li>
    <li><strong>Remaining budget</strong> — Save it. Put it toward a second light or a proper softbox once you understand what your current setup is missing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This single-key-plus-reflector approach is exactly the philosophy behind our <a href="/best-youtube-lighting-setup-budget-pro">best YouTube lighting setup on a budget</a> breakdown, which walks through scaling this same setup as your channel grows. And once your lighting is dialed in, the next bottleneck for most beginners is usually the camera itself — if you're still deciding on a body to pair with this setup, see our <a href="/best-camera-for-youtube-vlogging-india-2026">best camera for YouTube vlogging in India</a> guide.</p>

  <div class="callout">Common beginner mistake: buying the light before understanding placement. A single CRI-95 panel placed at 45 degrees to your subject, slightly above eye level, with a reflector bouncing fill from the shadow side, will consistently outperform a "3-light kit" placed carelessly. Read up on <a href="/three-point-lighting-explained">three-point lighting</a> before you even unbox your new light.</div>

  <h2>Signs a Cheap Light Is Not Worth Buying</h2>
  <p>A few red flags to watch for when browsing budget lighting listings, regardless of brand:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No CRI or TLCI number mentioned anywhere in the listing or manual.</li>
    <li>Marketing copy that only talks about lux/brightness and nothing about color accuracy.</li>
    <li>Reviews mentioning a visible green or magenta color cast on skin.</li>
    <li>A stand rated for a lighter fixture than the one it's bundled with — check the weight rating.</li>
    <li>No dimming control, or dimming that visibly shifts color temperature as you adjust it (a sign of cheap driver electronics).</li>
  </ul>

  <FAQBlock items={[
    {q: "Is a single light enough for beginner YouTube videos?", a: "Yes. One good bi-color LED or COB key light combined with a reflector for fill covers the vast majority of talking-head and interview-style content. Add a second light only once you've identified a specific gap, like needing a background separation light."},
    {q: "What CRI should I look for in a budget lighting kit?", a: "Aim for CRI 90 or higher, and treat CRI 95+ as ideal for skin tones. Below CRI 90, you'll often see a visible color cast on faces that's difficult to correct in post."},
    {q: "Should I buy a fixed daylight light or a bi-color one?", a: "Bi-color (adjustable roughly 3200K to 5600K) is worth the small extra cost because it lets you match window light or indoor practicals without gels. Most decent options under ₹15,000 are already bi-color."},
    {q: "Is a COB light better than an LED panel for beginners?", a: "Not necessarily better, just different. COB lights give more directional, shapeable light and grow with you via Bowens-mount modifiers, but LED panels are more portable and beginner-friendly straight out of the box."},
    {q: "Do I need a softbox with my first light?", a: "Not immediately. A cheap 5-in-1 reflector can soften and bounce your key light effectively while you're learning. Add a proper softbox once you understand how much diffusion your specific setup needs."}
  ]} />

